'''unbroiden†''', ''a.'' [< ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''broiden'', braided: see ME. ''braid'', ''broid''.] Unbraided.
{{smaller block|<poem>Hire myghty tresses of hire sonnysshe heres,
''Unbroiden'', hangen al aboute hire eeres.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 817.}}}}

'''unbroke†''' (un-brōk′), ''a.'' Unbroken.
{{smaller block|God keep all vows ''unbroke'' that swear to thee!
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., iv. 1. 215.}}
By age ''unbroke.'' {{right|''Pope'', Odyssey, viii. 147.}}}}

'''unbroken''' (un-brō′kn), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not broken; whole; entire; hence, left in its integrity; not violated.
{{smaller block|The clergy met very punctually, and the patriarch’s letter was produced in the assembly, the seal examined, and declared to be the patriarch’s and ''unbroken.''
{{right|''Bruce'', Source of the Nile, II. 460.}}}}
'''2.''' Not weakened; not crushed; not subdued: as, a mind ''unbroken'' by age.—'''3.''' Not tamed or rendered tractable; not taught; not accustomed to the saddle, harness, or yoke: as, an ''unbroken'' horse or ox.—'''4.''' Not interrupted; undisturbed: as, ''unbroken'' slumbers; hence, regular.
{{smaller block|The allied army returned to Lambeque unpursued and in ''unbroken'' order. {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xix.}}}}

'''unbrokenly''' (un-brō′kn-li), ''adv.'' Without break or intermission.

